<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Satyendranath Tagore</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1842">1842</a>–<a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Satyendranath Tagore was the first Indian to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a>, selected in 1863 and serving mainly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> Presidency until 1897. An elder brother of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a>, he was also a writer, translator and composer and a member of the Brahmo Samaj. He supported the education and social freedom of women, encouraging his wife Jnanadanandini Devi in reforms of dress and public life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/satyendranath-tagore.jpg" alt="Satyendranath Tagore" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Satyendranath_Tagore.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
